### FEATURE_NOTIFICATION — one person's own inbox, and nobody else's
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects. The demo backend runs on 8081 — Tomcat9 owns 8080
### on this machine.
###
### **Read the absences in this file as carefully as the requests.** There are five routes and there is
### no sixth, because four of the things a notification API usually offers are refused by name:
###
###   - **No route takes a person.** Not `?personId=`, not `/people/{id}/notifications`. An inbox is a
###     record of what somebody was chased about — which work, how often, how far up the ladder it
###     climbed — and read by a manager it is a performance history containing only the moments
###     something went wrong (`NOTIFICATION_02` §2). The absence is the enforcement, and
###     `NoRouteServesAnotherPersonsInboxTest` is where it is asserted rather than intended.
###   - **No mark-all-read.** It is the control people press instead of reading, and a channel that is
###     bulk-dismissed has already failed. If volume is high enough to want it, the fix is fewer
###     notifications.
###   - **No filter, no search, no grouping by kind.** A list short enough to need none of those is the
###     design goal.
###   - **No escalation switch**, in the preferences payload or anywhere else. Not a disabled toggle —
###     absent. A disabled control implies a permission that could be granted, and this one cannot.
###
### **AUTOMATION has no file here because it has no endpoint at all.** All six of its use cases say so:
### it is invoked by the scheduler and hands its findings to this feature through `NotifyUseCase`.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@manager = ionut@atelier.ro
@employee = andrei@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### Copy a real identifier out of the inbox response into this.
@notificationId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Sign in. `NOTIFICATION_VIEW_OWN` is granted to all three roles in V67 — there is no tier here,
### because everybody has an inbox and nobody has anybody else's.
# @name signIn
POST {{host}}/api/auth/sessions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{employee}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###

@csrf = {{signIn.response.headers.X-CSRF-TOKEN}}

### The inbox — unread first, then newest first, read rows dimmed rather than removed.
###
### A row whose `items` is non-empty is a **digest**: more notices released at one instant than the
### workspace's `digest_threshold`, folded into one expandable line. Escalations are never inside one —
### they are extracted and delivered individually however many there are.
###
### A row whose `subjectId` is null is one whose subject has gone. It renders as *This is no longer
### available*, never as stale text describing a task that has since changed. No message body is stored
### anywhere, so the sentence is composed by the screen from `kind` and nothing has travelled past the
### access checks that govern the original.
GET {{host}}/api/notifications

### The bell's number. A plain count, and the screen renders nothing at all when it is zero — the bell
### stays, the number goes. No red dot, no pulse, no growth: those are pressure, and pressure applied to
### a notification list produces bulk dismissal, which takes the escalations with it.
GET {{host}}/api/notifications/unread-count

### Open one. Two acts, one gesture: it is marked read and the screen goes to the subject. A separate
### acknowledge step is a step people stop taking, and then the unread count reports a backlog nobody
### has.
###
### Idempotent — the first instant is the true one, and pressing again does not move it.
###
### Try it with somebody else's identifier and the answer is **404, the same as one that does not
### exist**. Distinguishing them would confirm that a colleague was chased about something.
POST {{host}}/api/notifications/{{notificationId}}/read
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrf}}

### My four switches. Absent from storage means enabled, so somebody who has never opened the screen
### gets four `true`s back.
GET {{host}}/api/notification-preferences

### Switch off assignment notices. **Disabling a group stops creation, not just delivery** — no row is
### written for a suppressed notice, so nothing anywhere is counting what somebody declined.
###
### **And disabling never hides a marker.** At-risk, blocked and stalled are derived and render on every
### screen regardless. A person who mutes loses the prompt, not the information, which is what makes
### muting a reasonable choice rather than a way to hide from work.
PUT {{host}}/api/notification-preferences
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrf}}

{
  "assignment": false,
  "time": true,
  "process": true,
  "weekly": true
}

### The refusal worth seeing. There is no `escalation` field, and sending one is **422
### NOT_DISABLEABLE** rather than a silent 204 — a client told it succeeded would render a control that
### appeared to work, and a person would believe they had silenced the one mechanism that cannot be
### silenced. `V67`'s check constraint refuses the row as well, so the same rule holds one layer below
### anything an endpoint could do.
PUT {{host}}/api/notification-preferences
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{csrf}}

{
  "assignment": true,
  "time": true,
  "process": true,
  "weekly": true,
  "escalation": false
}

### Walking the hold and the re-check by hand, which is the only way to see the feature's argument.
###
### 1. Set the workspace's working hours to a window that has already closed today.
### 2. As the owner, assign the employee a task. Nothing arrives — `GET /api/notifications` as the
###    employee is unchanged, and the row sits in `notification` with `state = 'HELD'` and a
###    `deliver_after` at the next working instant.
### 3. As the employee, accept that task. The work was never hidden; it was always on their list.
### 4. Wait for the release pass — a minute, or `flowops.notification.release-pass-ms` — or advance
###    past `deliver_after`.
### 5. The row is now `CANCELLED` with `cancel_reason = 'SUBJECT_ALREADY_ACTED_ON'`, and the employee's
###    inbox is still empty.
###
### **That is the whole feature.** A prompt to do something already done is noise, and noise is what
### teaches a person to mute a channel — a lesson that does not reverse.
